KosmicKrisp: fewer Metal command buffers, allocators kept by size

Measured with Metal 4 directly: every MTL4CommandBuffer begun on an allocator
costs ~2.3 MB of it, whatever it holds (30 command buffers of 100 draws on
one allocator: 69.6 MB, +117 MB of process footprint once executed; one of
3000 draws: 2.4 MB), and an allocator never gives memory back. Counter-Strike
2 made ~9,700 Metal command buffers a second (~72 a frame): the shared
command buffer of the render passes was closed at nearly every barrier
between two passes (kk_cmd_buffer_dependency), in case work hoisted before a
pass (pre_gfx: predicates, unrolling, tessellation) would come after it. The
allocators those command buffers grew reached ~90 MB, the LIFO pool (256 MB)
was full after three, and ~800 of them were made and destroyed every 5 s --
29 GB of allocator memory released per 5 s, the Metal4 dispatch queue
finalizing IOGPU resources while the render thread waited on the GPU for
1.3 s (stage 61, sections 8-9).

- kk_cmd_buffer_dependency only notes the dependency (dep_since_open); a draw
  that hoists work after one splits the pass there (kk_draw ->
  kk_cmd_split_render_pass, before the encoder is used). KK_EAGER_DEP_CLOSE=1
  closes at the barrier as before.
- Allocators: a command buffer asks for the sizes its last recording needed
  (alloc_hint) and gets the smallest idle allocator at least that large;
  when the pool (now 768 MB) is full the allocator unused the longest goes.
- KK_ALLOC_LOG=1: allocators made/reused/released and Metal command buffers
  made, every 5 s.

Counter-Strike 2, Dust2 with bots, same settings: 84 -> 133 fps average, 1%
low 8 -> 54, 0.1% low 1.5 -> 14, p99 35 -> 12.5 ms, frames over 100 ms 18
-> 5 in 120 s; Metal command buffers 9,700/s -> 3,900/s; allocators made
800/5 s -> 0.
- Metal's compiler service may run as many compilations at once as it can
  (setShouldMaximizeConcurrentCompilation, as CodeWeavers' DXMT does): a
  burst of new pipelines from Source 2's job threads compiled one after
  another (KK_MAX_CONCURRENT_COMPILE=0: Metal's default).
- KK_ALLOC_LOG=1 also prints the GPU busy time of the commits per 5 s (from
  Metal's commit feedback): whether a slow frame rate is the GPU's.

